  "intro": "When $f$ is built from sums, products, quotients, powers, and roots of continuous pieces, you can usually evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to a} f(x)$ by direct substitution. The limit laws let you split a limit across these operations. Substitution fails when it produces the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$; then you first simplify, by factoring and canceling, by rationalizing with a conjugate, or by combining fractions, and substitute into the simplified expression.",
  "items": [
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 1,
      "prompt": "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\,(2x^2 - 4x + 1)$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Polynomials are continuous, so substitute $x=3$ directly.",
          "$2(3)^2 - 4(3) + 1 = 18 - 12 + 1$.",
          "Combine: $18 - 12 + 1 = 7$."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\lim_{x\\to 3}(2x^2-4x+1)=7$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 2,
      "prompt": "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\dfrac{x^2-25}{x-5}$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Direct substitution gives $\\frac{0}{0}$, an indeterminate form, so simplify first.",
          "Factor the numerator: $x^2-25=(x-5)(x+5)$.",
          "Cancel the common factor: $\\frac{(x-5)(x+5)}{x-5}=x+5$ for $x\\ne 5$.",
          "Substitute into the simplified form: $5+5=10$."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\dfrac{x^2-25}{x-5}=10$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 3,
      "prompt": "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x}$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Direct substitution gives $\\frac{0}{0}$, so rationalize the numerator.",
          "Multiply by the conjugate: $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x}\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+4}+2}{\\sqrt{x+4}+2}=\\dfrac{(x+4)-4}{x(\\sqrt{x+4}+2)}$.",
          "Simplify the numerator to $x$ and cancel: $\\dfrac{x}{x(\\sqrt{x+4}+2)}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x+4}+2}$.",
          "Substitute $x=0$: $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{4}+2}=\\dfrac{1}{4}$."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x}=\\dfrac{1}{4}$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "practice",
      "id": 4,
      "prompt": "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\dfrac{x^2+x-6}{x-2}$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Substitution gives $\\frac{0}{0}$, so factor: $x^2+x-6=(x-2)(x+3)$.",
          "Cancel $x-2$ to get $x+3$, then substitute $x=2$: $2+3=5$."
        ],
        "answer": "$5$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "practice",
      "id": 5,
      "prompt": "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 4}\\dfrac{2x+1}{x-1}$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "The denominator is not zero at $x=4$, so substitute directly.",
          "$\\dfrac{2(4)+1}{4-1}=\\dfrac{9}{3}=3$."
        ],
        "answer": "$3$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "practice",
      "id": 6,
      "prompt": "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\dfrac{x^3-8}{x-2}$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Substitution gives $\\frac{0}{0}$; factor the difference of cubes $x^3-8=(x-2)(x^2+2x+4)$.",
          "Cancel $x-2$ and substitute $x=2$: $2^2+2(2)+4=12$."
        ],
        "answer": "$12$"
      }
    }
